                  {
                    "value": "Finding: why the United Kingdom is at 43%",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontFamily": "Segoe UI Semibold",
                      "fontSize": "11pt",
                      "color": "#0b0b0b"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "Tree: the UK has the largest negative gap (−$5.8M). Split by rep: Jae Pak −$10.1M (his whole UK quota, no UK sales), José Saraiva +$3.8M, Amy Alberts +$0.4M.",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontFamily": "Segoe UI",
                      "fontSize": "9pt",
                      "color": "#52514e"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "Rep detail: Jae Pak's home territory is the UK, but all 6,738 of his order lines are booked to Canada ($8.5M). José Saraiva, based in Canada, books $3.8M in the UK.",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontFamily": "Segoe UI",
                      "fontSize": "9pt",
                      "color": "#52514e"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "So the UK quota follows Jae Pak while his sales land in Canada: a territory-assignment artefact in the source data, not UK resellers buying less. Fix the assignment before coaching anyone.",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontFamily": "Segoe UI",
                      "fontSize": "9pt",
                      "color": "#52514e"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
